#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ryzenadj-thermal — hold the 5600H below its thermal cliff.
#
# The problem. This is an HP Pavilion Gaming 15-ec2xxx: Ryzen 5 5600H plus an
# RTX 3050 Mobile sharing one modest heatpipe assembly. The firmware's own
# junction-temperature target is Tjmax, 95°C, and it will happily drive the part
# there and sit on it. Measured at the desktop — no game, just a long-lived node
# process, dockerd and a browser — Tctl read 94.6°C, then 96.25°C, with all
# twelve threads at 4.09 GHz. The NVIDIA side agreed: nvidia-smi's
# SW Thermal Slowdown counter had accumulated 2.86 hours over a 49-hour uptime,
# and the GPU idled at 72°C while drawing 9 W of its 60 W budget.
#
# The tell that this is airflow rather than heat transport: idle sits at 74.8°C
# and a pinned all-core load only reaches 85.4°C. A cooler with a bad die
# interface shows a steep rise under load. This one is already saturated before
# the load arrives and barely moves — the whole curve is shifted up, which is
# what a fan that never leaves its lowest speed produces.
#
# Running at Tjmax is not a safe steady state to design around. The part is not
# in danger — it protects itself — but "protects itself" means it throttles, and
# throttling is exactly the symptom being chased. Worse, it throttles reactively:
# it overshoots, cuts hard, recovers, overshoots again. The clock trace sawtooths
# and frametimes go with it.
#
# What this does. It lowers the junction-temperature target and the sustained
# power budget, so the part stabilises just under the cliff instead of bouncing
# off it. The trade is real and worth stating plainly: peak single-core boost
# drops a little. Sustained all-core clocks usually RISE, because the part stops
# spending its time in recovery. On a chassis that cannot carry stock limits,
# capping is faster than not capping.
#
# This is a mitigation, not a fix. It is worth being precise about what the
# actual fault is, because the obvious guess was wrong. Thermal paste is NOT the
# problem — this machine was repasted a month before these measurements. The
# fault is airflow: the fans turn, but the stock curve never ramps them, so they
# idle quietly while the part cooks. hp-wmi cannot see or change this (its fan
# inputs read a flat 0 and its boost mode moved a pinned all-core load by 0.8°C).
# nbfc-linux drives the EC directly and is the real fix — see meta/packages.txt.
#
# ORDER MATTERS. Do not enable this unit until nbfc_service is working. With the
# fans unfixed this machine idles at Tctl 74.8°C, so a cap of 85°C leaves barely
# ten degrees and would throttle the part at rest — trading a heat problem for a
# permanent performance problem. Fix the airflow, re-measure idle and load, then
# set TCTL from what the machine actually does.
#
# TUNING. The values below are a conservative starting point, NOT this machine's
# measured stock limits — they were chosen before ryzenadj was installed. Run
# `sudo ryzenadj -i` to read what the firmware actually offers, then adjust.
# TCTL is the setting that matters most; the wattages are secondary. If games
# feel sluggish rather than merely cooler, raise STAPM first, then TCTL.
#
# Not installable by stow: it belongs to root under /usr/local/bin. See the
# service file for the install and enable sequence.

set -euo pipefail

# Junction temperature ceiling, °C. Stock is 95 (Tjmax).
#
# This was 85 while the fans were still stuck at their lowest speed, when the
# machine idled at 74.8°C and the cap was the only thing standing between it and
# Tjmax. With nbfc-linux driving the fans properly that reasoning no longer
# holds: idle fell to the mid 60s, so an 85°C ceiling is no longer a safety net,
# it is just a clamp that would bite mid-game for no reason. 90 keeps five
# degrees clear of Tjmax — enough to stop the reactive sawtooth — while leaving
# the part room to actually boost now that the heat can get out.
TCTL=90

# Sustained power budget, mW. STAPM is the long-run average the firmware targets.
STAPM=35000

# Short-burst ceiling, mW — what a single-threaded spike may draw for a moment.
FAST=45000

# Medium-window ceiling, mW, between FAST and STAPM.
SLOW=38000

ryzenadj=/usr/bin/ryzenadj
[ -x "$ryzenadj" ] || { echo "ryzenadj not present at $ryzenadj" >&2; exit 1; }

# --tctl-temp is the one that does the real work here; the power limits keep the
# part from sprinting into the ceiling in the first place. Applying them in one
# invocation matters — ryzenadj writes them as a set.
"$ryzenadj" \
    --tctl-temp="$TCTL" \
    --stapm-limit="$STAPM" \
    --fast-limit="$FAST" \
    --slow-limit="$SLOW"

echo "applied: tctl=${TCTL}C stapm=${STAPM}mW fast=${FAST}mW slow=${SLOW}mW"
